Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Abstract", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9806706827369204`*^9, 
  3.980670685410715*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"63c94001-d7a5-4c28-b546-\
648708605c4e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "In this notebook, we re-analyze the inverted spherical pendulum in new \
terms: as a gauge theory in ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"O", "(", "3", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "f09a10d8-75ec-4239-8be7-d9d729cb3607"],
 ", the Lie group of rotations in 3 dimensions. We first reproduce the \
equations of motion in pitch/cone-roll coordinates, known from earlier \
notebooks, from connections in the covariant derivative, computed \
automatically from a kinetic-energy metric. This metric measures distances \
between configuration velocities in the 2D quotient group ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"O", "(", "3", ")"}], "/", "S"}], "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"O", "(", "2", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "2047dd8b-06ff-457b-905c-5603aa27e2ad"],
 ". Adding a third angle \[Psi], spinning the pendulum on its long axis, \
extends the group via ",
 StyleBox["fibration",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"O", "(", "3", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5207448b-ca93-4df8-9b8b-7bd890cd38e9"],
 ". Physically, it adds gyroscopic forces to the pendulum, making it easier \
to stabilize than by, say, control forces applied at the base. We offer an \
interactive visualization that lets a user smoothly vary the system from a \
free pendulum that falls, to a gyroscope that precesses and nutates. The \
visualization also reveals coordinate singularities, which we eliminate in an \
upcoming notebook that addresses the problem in another gauge, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"S", "\[VeryThinSpace]", 
    RowBox[{"U", "(", "2", ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "24277e03-14c3-4ebb-8426-6243262e1dec"],
 ",  the unitary group of spinors or quaternions, the exact same group used \
in quantum theory of the Standard Model."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9806706908995934`*^9, 3.9806714020026007`*^9}, {
   3.980671434066988*^9, 3.980671650330995*^9}, {3.9806729546032457`*^9, 
   3.980673090750882*^9}, {3.980765390924571*^9, 3.9807654956023607`*^9}, {
   3.980804234250622*^9, 3.98080437664402*^9}, {3.980804489450246*^9, 
